art, but we brought them in and said, well, can we adapt this and develop programs that would be really targeting the children who are in the hospital? And they delivered some amazing programs across our pediatric wards. So even as you were seeing the effects that the arts were having on patients, you were also noticing that among some of your colleagues, doctors and administrators, they saw this as a form of entertainment, not as a form of therapy. Can you talk about this disconnect that you've experienced, Daisy? Well, it was a sort of a situation. where if people didn't know what it was and you started describing it, it was sort of described as, oh, yeah, the hospital entertainment program. But actually I noticed that as soon as we started
艺术，但我们把他们带进来，说，好吧，我们能调整一下并开发一些真正针对医院里孩子的项目吗？他们在我们的儿科病房开展了一些很棒的项目。所以，即使你在观察艺术对病人产生的影响，你也注意到在你的同事、医生和行政人员中，他们把这视为一种娱乐，而不是一种治疗。你能谈谈你所经历的这种脱节吗？嗯，这是一种情况。

